  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the coral holobiont as a metaorganism made up of the coral host and a diverse microbiome, all interacting within the surrounding reef environment. At the center is the coral animal, linked by two-way arrows to major partners including Symbiodiniaceae, bacteria, viruses, fungi, archaea, other protists, and endolithic algae. These arrows indicate exchanges and functional relationships rather than a simple linear pathway.\n\nA key message is that nutrients move among these partners in different forms, especially carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, and sulfur, shown by small labeled boxes near each group. Symbiodiniaceae and endolithic algae appear to be major carbon-related contributors, reflecting photosynthetic input, while bacteria are associated with all four nutrient types, suggesting a broad role in cycling and transformation. Archaea are highlighted mainly for nitrogen, fungi for phosphorus, and viruses sit centrally as agents influencing multiple interactions.\n\nThe legend indicates the microbiome’s roles in coral health: providing shelter and protection, transferring nutrients and photosynthate, contributing to organic carbon production, cycling nutrients, suppressing pathogens, and modulating microbial populations through gene transfer and antimicrobial activity. Overall, the image portrays the coral as an integrated ecological network rather than a standalone organism.",
